### 1. Source control: who can set `serverUrl`?

`getServerUrl()` reads the value from `chrome.storage.local`:

```js
// gen/ext_bg_module.js:9-22
function getServerUrl(callback) {
  objStorageGet({serverUrl: null}, function (val) {
    var value = val.serverUrl;
    if (value) { callback(value); }
    else { callback('https://meeting.is' /* or staging */); }
  });
}
```

`serverUrl` is **not** only a developer constant. It is attacker-settable via the external message listener:

```js
// gen/ext_bg_module.js:122-143
chrome.runtime.onMessageExternal.addListener(function (req, sender, callback) {
  ...
  } else if (req.cmd == 'setServerUrl') {
    // Although an arbitrary URL can be set, message passing only works with the
    // sites listed in manifest.json...
    setServerUrl(req.url);          // -> chrome.storage.local.set({serverUrl: req.url})
    return false;
  }
```

There is **no `sender.origin` check, no `sender.id` allowlist, and no URL scheme/validation** on `req.url`. The comment explicitly acknowledges an arbitrary URL can be set. The only restriction is `externally_connectable.matches: ["https://*.cran
